# Local configuration — copy .env.example to .env and edit.
.env

# OSM extracts. The France extract alone is ~4.8 GB; the filtered and carved
# ones are regenerated by `make build_osm_toll_booths`.
*.osm.pbf

# OSRM graphs, when OSRM_OPTIMAL_PATH points inside the working tree
# (~9 GB per instance).
/.data/

# Regenerable intermediates under data/. The deliverables next to them —
# osm_toll_booths.yaml, route_set.yaml, vinci_toll_booth.yaml, vinci_price.yaml
# — are committed on purpose, so a consumer can read them without running the
# pipeline.
#
# The four reports are not. They are diagnostics about a particular run rather
# than published outputs: excluded_booths.json says which booths the through-road
# rule dropped and why, uncovered_booths.json says which coverage targets no
# route reached, segment_weights_report.json carries every segment's samples and
# every pair's reconstruction, and price_survey.json is what --survey writes on a diagnostic
# run. The first two are produced on every run — build_vinci_toll_booths reads
# excluded_booths.json to name the nearest excluded booth when a placemark
# matches nothing — they are simply not carried in the repository.
#
# endpoint_snap.json is a cache, not a report: 25 MB of how far the router puts
# the start of a trip from each of a million place nodes. It is derived from the
# extract and the routing graph, both of which stay out of the repository, so
# carrying it here would commit a measurement no one could check.
/data/.builder-image.stamp
/data/plaza_cells.geojson
/data/excluded_booths.json
/data/uncovered_booths.json
/data/endpoint_snap.json
/data/price_survey.json
/data/segment_weights_report.json

# The rejection database is a measurement, not a deliverable: 260k routing
# decisions taken against the OSRM graph, which stays out of the repository.
# Carrying it here would commit a result no one could check. Rebuilding costs
# ~7 min of routing.
/data/vinci_routes_rejected.sqlite3

__pycache__/
